INT. CUCKOO CLOCK REPAIR SHOP - EVENING

A narrow shop in a Bavarian alley. Hundreds of carved clocks ticking out of sync. Pendulums swing like drunken metronomes. A brass lamp hangs over a workbench strewn with tiny gears.

HORATIO PIMM (60s, a retired detective in a moth-eaten waistcoat, magnifier on his forehead) bends over a gutted clock, humming. MISS LAVINIA GRAY (50s, gray bun, gloves buttoned to the wrist) sits primly in the customer's chair. DR. EUSTACE KROLL (40s, doctor's bag, thick glasses, nervous smile) paces behind her.

HORATIO
It's a charming movement, Miss Gray. Eighteen-forty, I'd say.

LAVINIA
It was my husband's.

HORATIO
Ah. Then it has seen some history.

EUSTACE
Miss Gray, we really should be getting on.

LAVINIA
Doctor, I'm in no hurry.

He stops. Checks his watch. Puts it away.

HORATIO
Tell me, Miss Gray. Your husband. When did he die?

LAVINIA
Last Thursday.

HORATIO
And you've come to repair his clock so soon.

LAVINIA
He asked me to.

HORATIO
Did he?

Horatio lifts a tiny brass cog from the clock. Turns it in the lamplight.

HORATIO (CONT'D)
This isn't a clock part. It's a lock. A pharmacy lock.

Eustace goes pale. Lavinia does not move. The ticking seems to grow louder.

EUSTACE
Inspector, I can explain.

HORATIO
I'm retired. But go on.

Eustace lunges for the door. Lavinia whips a hatpin from her sleeve. Horatio ducks under the workbench. The doctor trips on the rug and slams into a grandfather clock.

All the clocks begin to chime at once. Cuckoos burst out of every window.

In the din, Horatio rises, hatpin quivering in the wood a hand's width from his ear. Lavinia and Eustace face each other, panting. Both look at the clocks. Both look at him.

HORATIO (CONT'D)
Eight o'clock. Right on time.

LAVINIA
You fool. He was dying anyway.

HORATIO
Yes. But not on Thursday.
